# Retinal OCT Anomaly Detection with Transfer Learning

A binary image classification system that detects abnormal findings (CNV, DME, DRUSEN) versus normal retinal tissue in Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) scans, using transfer learning on a pretrained ResNet18 model.

## Overview

Optical Coherence Tomography is a core imaging modality in ophthalmology, used to detect retinal diseases such as choroidal neovascularization (CNV), diabetic macular edema (DME), and drusen deposits. Manual review of OCT scans is time-consuming and requires specialist expertise. This project explores whether a lightweight, transfer-learning-based deep learning model can flag abnormal scans for further review, as a proof-of-concept toward AI-assisted screening in ophthalmology.

## Dataset

- **Source:** [Kermany et al. OCT dataset](https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rscbjbr9sj/3) (also available via Kaggle), a large public collection of labeled retinal OCT images across four categories: CNV, DME, DRUSEN, and NORMAL.
- **Task framing:** The three abnormal categories (CNV, DME, DRUSEN) were merged into a single `abnormal` class and contrasted against `normal`, framing the problem as binary anomaly detection.
- **Split used:**
  - Train: 400 images per class (1200 abnormal, 400 normal)
  - Validation: 80 images per class (240 abnormal, 80 normal), drawn from the dataset's held-out test split to avoid leakage

## Method

- **Architecture:** ResNet18, pretrained on ImageNet, with the convolutional backbone frozen and a new single-output linear layer trained on top (transfer learning).
- **Framework:** PyTorch (`torchvision` for data loading and the pretrained model).
- **Preprocessing:** Images resized to 224x224 and converted to tensors via `torchvision.transforms`.
- **Loss / optimizer:** `BCEWithLogitsLoss`, `Adam` optimizer on the final layer's parameters only.
- **Training:** 3 epochs, batch size 16, with per-epoch loss and accuracy averaged over all batches (not just the last batch) for an honest training signal.

## Results

Evaluated on the held-out validation set (320 images):

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Overall accuracy | 90.94% |
| Precision (abnormal) | 91.37% |
| Recall (abnormal) | 97.08% |
| F1-score (abnormal) | 94.14% |

**Confusion matrix** (rows = true label, columns = predicted label):

|  | Predicted: abnormal | Predicted: normal |
|---|---|---|
| **True: abnormal** | 233 | 7 |
| **True: normal** | 22 | 58 |

Recall was prioritized as a key metric given the clinical context: missing a true abnormal case (false negative) is more costly than a false alarm. The model missed only 7 of 240 abnormal cases in validation.

The trained model was also tested manually on unseen single images (outside the train/val split) and correctly classified them.

## Usage

```bash
pip install torch torchvision scikit-learn pillow
```

1. Download the Kermany OCT dataset and update the source path in `prepare_data.py`.
2. Run `prepare_data.py` once to build the `data/train` and `data/val` folders.
3. Run `OCT1.py` to train the model and save weights to `oct_model.pth`.
4. Run `test_single_image.py` (with an image path set) to classify a single new image.

## Project structure

```
prepare_data.py       # Samples and organizes images from the raw dataset into train/val folders
OCT1.py                # Builds the model, trains it, evaluates on validation set, saves weights
test_single_image.py   # Loads the saved model and classifies a single new OCT image
oct_model.pth           # Saved trained model weights
```

## Limitations & next steps

- The training set (1600 images) is a small subset of the full Kermany dataset (which contains over 100,000 images); results may not generalize as well as a model trained on the full dataset.
- The binary framing (normal vs. abnormal) collapses three distinct pathologies into one class; a multi-class model (CNV vs. DME vs. DRUSEN vs. NORMAL) would be more clinically informative.
- Only the final layer was fine-tuned; unfreezing deeper layers of ResNet18 with a larger dataset (e.g. on GPU via Google Colab) could improve performance further.
- No formal test-set holdout beyond validation; a three-way train/val/test split would give a less biased final performance estimate.